There is one generic set of problems with Nethack (and Larn, and probably
Moria, though I don't remember, and come to that with most commercial
adventures).  It always annoys me, but I would hope that this posting will
be taken as constructive suggestions rather than as flaming, since I do
enjoy the games.  I categorize them as 'simple unreasonableness' or as
'unrealistic ignorance (on the part of the character)'.  Examples (some
of which have been mentioned already)...

1) Not being able to tell if a d/f is your pet or a hostile one until
you've let it get close enough to hit you.  (Don't know about you, but
I'd *mark* my pet for instant identification.)

2) Walking into closed doors.  Unless I'm blind, surely I can see if there
is a door in a doorway or not.  (Here it's particularly the 'ouch, you
walk into a door' that gets to me.  I can accept that there might not
be enough symbols to give separate ones to open/closed doors.)

3) Not being able to tell that a 'short sword' (or whatever it is) is a
mattock until you identify it.  Surely an adventurer could recognize
weapon type on sight, even if he couldn't tell other characteristics
like dwarvish or elfin...

4) Not stopping 'multi-time step' actions (like eat or 10.) until a monster
actually comes and hits you.  I'd certainly stop as soon as one came in
the door to the room.  (Maybe searching is an exception since that takes
attention, but certainly eating and resting.)  (And, as someone else
mentioned, if I was eating, I'd *drop* my food, not take time to stash
it in my pack.)

5) From 'The Pawn', just to show that commercial offerings do it as well,
there's a room full of boards with a door that needs to be closed but
can't be.  Experimenting eventually shows that you need to move the boards
because they are in the way of the door.  Yet surely, I should be able to
see *as soon as I try to close the door* that the boards are blocking it.

I'm sure that with a bit of thought you can probably come up with hundreds
more of these illogicalities in most games, and as I say I find them
slightly annoying (or sometimes boring).  I'd like to see them removed
from the game-writing universe.

